# Local vs cloud agent coordination

**Local Cursor IDE agents only.** Cloud agents auto-pick GitHub issues labeled `agent-queue`, skip any issue labeled `blocked`, and open PRs from `cursor/…` branches into `staging`. Do not run this claim protocol on a `cursor/` branch.

Before starting work on any issue or epic, do these steps in order. For an epic, include every sub-issue (`gh issue view <epic> --json subIssues --jq '.subIssues[].number'`).

## 1. Check labels, open PRs, and `cursor/*` branches

```bash
gh issue view <n> --json number,title,state,labels,subIssues
gh pr list --state open --limit 100 --json number,title,body,headRefName,url
git ls-remote --heads origin 'refs/heads/cursor/*'
```

A cloud agent already has `<n>` when either is true:

- an open PR whose head ref starts with `cursor/` and whose title or body references `#<n>`
- a remote `cursor/*` branch whose name includes `<n>`, or whose tip commit message references `#<n>` (fetch that ref, then `git log -1 --format=%s`)

## 2. Stop when a cloud agent has it

Do not edit labels, branch, or commit. Tell the user the issue number and the open PR and/or remote `cursor/*` branch you found.

## 3. Otherwise claim with `blocked`

Create the label if it is missing, then add it to each issue you are taking (the epic and its sub-issues):

```bash
gh label list --json name --jq '.[].name' | grep -qx blocked \
  || gh label create blocked \
    --description "Claimed by a local Cursor agent; cloud agent-queue skips it" \
    --color B60205
gh issue edit <n> --add-label blocked
```

Remove `blocked` from those same issues when the local PR is opened, or if the work is abandoned:

```bash
gh issue edit <n> --remove-label blocked
```

## 4. Update `staging`, then use a non-`cursor/` branch

```bash
git checkout staging && git pull origin staging
git checkout -b feat/<issue#>-<kebab-slug>
```

Do not create a branch whose name starts with `cursor/`. That prefix belongs to cloud agents.
